    {
      "dat": "Data/PC/190852/ZZ_Win.dat",
      "dic": "genlocalisation/ww2/localisation/translations/pol/long_description_unite.dic",
      "entries": [
        {
          "key": "45209413f6958e05",
          "value": "Bigger, heavier and more expensive, that could be the motto of the ironically named Pz. VIII ''Maus'' (''Mouse''). Weighing 188 tons and with the thickest armor of the war, the twin 128mm and 75mm guns in its turret could pierce any armor even at very, very long range. However, as slow as it was powerful, it is an easy target for fighter-bombers and any infantry that can get close enough. Without an escort against those dual threats, the Maus cannot last long in its ''mobile bunker'' role."
        },
        {
          "key": "47209413f6958e05",
          "value": "In production from 1941 and built in greater numbers than any other British heavy tank, the A22 Churchill is best known for its almost unpierceable armor. It carried only an average 75mm gun, however, which was rather light to engage its opposite numbers. As a result it is best used as support for more powerful units or for the infantry. Its sturdiness lets the Churchill take many hits before being damaged, which allows more fragile units to take cover or hold out until support comes to their rescue."
        },
        {
          "key": "c2209413f6958e05",
          "value": "The world-famous Katyusha was the first multiple rocket-launcher vehicle in history. Built from the ZIS-6 truck, it carried sixteen 132mm rockets on its folding frame's rails, firing terrible barrages at a range equivalent to that of 75mm field guns. Lacking accuracy, it relies on its rockets' high explosive capacities to saturate an area, usually creating panic on a large scale. Though very mobile, the Katyusha is also very fragile."
        },
        {
          "key": "02219413f6958e05",
          "value": "A physical incarnation of the ''Blitzkrieg'' doctrine, the Panzerkampfwagen III J was fast as well as easy and cheap to produce, so the Wehrmacht fielded large numbers of them in a very short time. Though its main 50mm gun was only average and its armor was poor, the Panzer III remained the best in its category. An easy target for enemy medium tanks, it is still useful for hitting enemy infantry with its MG-34 machine-gun or striking at unprotected enemy flanks."
        },
        {
          "key": "44219413f6958e05",
          "value": "At a moderate cost, the Cruiser Mk. III was the first British tank to be truly effective on the battlefield. Built with a Christie suspension, a 12-cylinder engine and light armor (making it very light-weighted), it was a fast and maneuverable tank. In addition to its small size it had a relatively effective 2 pdr. (40mm) main gun. Of course, this kind of mobility has its drawbacks: the Cruiser is very vulnerable to enemy anti-tank weapons."
        },
        {
          "key": "47219413f6958e05",
          "value": "The sole Italian tank really deserving the title ''medium tank'', the M13/40 was inferior to its foreign counterparts in terms of armor though it could face enemy light tanks. Its turret carries the short 47/32 gun, a piece at home against light armour and increasingly out of its depth against anything heavier. It kept the quick-firing 8mm Breda machine-guns that made Italian tanks so deadly against infantry, and it is exceptionally fast for a medium tank: fall on a target, hit it, and be gone before help arrives."
        },
        {
          "key": "48219413f6958e05",
          "value": "A late-war Italian medium tank, and the point where the design finally caught up with the war. The turret carries the longer 47/40 gun in place of the short 47/32 of its predecessors, trading nothing to gain the penetration needed to face an enemy medium on even terms. Thicker plate and a petrol engine came with it. Still armed with HE shells and machine-guns, it crushes infantry and light units as easily as ever: fairly mobile, moderately priced once developed, and a sound long-term investment for the Italian army."
        },
        {
          "key": "48f5630343816123",
          "value": "A stopgap, and a good one. With the KV-1 outgunned by 1943, the Red Army cut open the fighting compartment of a lightened hull to take an 85mm gun in a new turret, and had a heavy tank that could fight the ones it kept meeting. Fewer than one hundred and fifty were built before better designs replaced it. The armour is still heavy and the speed still poor, but the gun is at last worth the weight carrying it. It is best used to lead an attack, with lighter tanks following through.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "0fb767d5fca9f329",
          "value": "The 90mm Model 39 was the finest gun Italy fielded in the war, an anti-aircraft piece that proved even more effective at destroying tanks. Its high velocity and long barrel let it defeat any armour it met. Its weakness is what it sits on: a heavy towed carriage. Expensive and slow to produce, it is best used to cover vital positions, ideally with infantry to screen it.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "8c5b8059cef2442b",
          "value": "The most produced British tank of the war, with more than eight thousand built and close to a third of them shipped to the Red Army, where its mechanical reliability was valued far above its modest performance. Built on a cruiser chassis but plated like an infantry tank, the Valentine trades speed for an armour thickness its small silhouette does not suggest. Its 2 pdr. (40mm) gun, however, is already outdated by the time it reaches the front. Cheap, sturdy and available early, it is at its best escorting infantry.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "b5dff04ee0af8c31",
          "value": "Designed in 1943 for the open ground of North Africa, the Carro Celere Sahariano borrowed the layout of the British cruisers it had been fighting: Christie suspension, a low hull and speed above all else. Armed with the long 47/40 gun, it was the fastest tank Italy ever designed, though a single prototype was all that was ever completed. It arrived too late for the kind of fighting it was conceived for, and its light armour will not survive a stand-up fight: it is best used to strike and withdraw.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "3c931f8d4ee48d36",
          "value": "The last and best of the Soviet fast tanks, more than five thousand of which were built before the German invasion. Its Christie suspension and enormous engine give it a road speed nothing in 1941 could match, and its 45mm gun was perfectly adequate for the tanks of the day. The armour, however, is barely proof against rifle fire, and it burns readily. It is best used as cavalry, flanking, raiding and exploiting a gap, and should never be sent into a stand-up fight.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "b3f4aa1c4a250467",
          "value": "The last and largest of the Italian heavy tank studies, sketched in the final months as an answer to the German and Soviet heavies: thicker armour, a longer high-velocity 90mm gun and a weight Italian industry had never attempted. No prototype was ever begun, and the project ended with the country's surrender. Its gun will defeat any tank it meets, but the vehicle is very expensive and slow to produce. Deployed in small numbers at the decisive point, it is the strongest armour Italy can field.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "a3bc6c8f18ba1772",
          "value": "The T-34 rearmed for the second half of the war. The new 85mm gun needed a larger three-man turret, which at last freed the commander from also serving the gun, the worst weakness of the original. Everything that made the T-34 formidable survives: the sloped armour, the mobility and the ability to be built by the thousand. It is still no match for the heaviest German armour head-on, but deployed in numbers it can meet almost anything else on equal terms.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "49ec079a096ddf79",
          "value": "The A43 was the Churchill taken to its logical end: the same slow, immensely thick hull, widened to carry a turret large enough for the 17 pounder. Six were built in 1945. The gun was the best anti-tank weapon Britain had and the armour will stop almost anything aimed at it, but the extra weight went onto a chassis that was already the slowest in the army. Too slow to manoeuvre, it is best used as a moving shield on a narrow front, destroying what comes forward to meet it.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "78734269dd3a4685",
          "value": "Adopted in 1944 and known to its crews as the beast killer, the BS-3 was the Red Army's answer to the heaviest German armour. A naval gun adapted to a field carriage, it throws a 100mm shell flat and fast enough to defeat any tank of the war at combat range. It is also enormous, well over three tons, expensive and slow to deploy, and it never arrived in the numbers its crews wanted. It is best kept for the routes that matter, with infantry in support.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "f0692e62770d9285",
          "value": "An Italian answer to the heavy tanks of 1943, the P43 was drawn around a 75mm gun in a cast turret and armour thicker than anything Italy had built. On paper it was a serious design, a match for the medium tanks its allies and its enemies were fielding, but the armistice came first and not one was completed. Well protected and reasonably mobile, it nonetheless lacks the armour penetration of the foreign guns of its year. It is best used as a battle tank among others rather than sent to duel heavy armour on its own.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "9eb5dfb80acb908e",
          "value": "An assault tank in all but name: the M4A3E2 is a Sherman with its frontal plate more than doubled and a much heavier turret, built in a hurry for the units that had to lead an attack down a road with no way round it. Barely two hundred and fifty were made. All that steel costs it speed, and the gun is unchanged, so it will not win a duel against a heavy tank. Its role is to go first and absorb what is aimed at the column, while the tanks behind it do the shooting.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "c6d19b86515c3b9a",
          "value": "The heavier of the Japanese super-heavy studies, developed from the multi-turreted O-I and meant to carry thicker armour and a heavier gun. Like everything in that programme it existed as drawings and a part-built hull, in a country whose industry could not have produced it in numbers. Enormous and very slow, armed to break fortifications rather than to duel, it can absorb an extraordinary amount of fire. Extremely expensive, it must be escorted against the aircraft and infantry it cannot fight off on its own.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "1b7f480cce415c9b",
          "value": "The Pak 43 is the anti-tank version of the long 88mm gun, designed from the start for that role, unlike the Flak 88. It uses the same ammunition as the King Tiger and can destroy any Allied tank at ranges where they cannot reply. Its drawbacks are those of every heavy towed gun: expensive, slow to produce and hard to conceal.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "97b003875e3ccd9d",
          "value": "The 90mm M3 was America's heavy anti-aircraft gun, and like every gun of its class it soon found itself pointing at tanks instead. On a towed anti-tank carriage from 1943 it defeats the frontal armour of almost anything it meets, at very long range, and it remains easy enough to conceal in rough terrain to lay an ambush. It is expensive, however, slow to bring into battery and unable to move once the shooting has started. It is best sited on a road the enemy must use, with infantry in support.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "4be6ac2cf65554a3",
          "value": "The definitive version of the Sturmgeschutz and the most produced German armoured vehicle of the war, with close to ten thousand built. Its long 75mm gun is the same one that arms the Panzer IV, but mounted in a casemate, which gives the StuG a very low silhouette and an affordable price. Without a turret, however, it must turn its whole hull to answer a threat from the flank.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "da31cc8f590c0db1",
          "value": "The M4A3E8, universally known as the Easy Eight, is the Sherman as it should have been from the start. Its wider-tracked suspension smooths the ride enough to shoot accurately on the move, and the long 76mm gun will go through what the old 75mm could not. The armour is unchanged, however, and still thin for 1945. It wins by shooting first rather than by absorbing hits, and like every Sherman it is best deployed in numbers.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "83f820631ef17eb9",
          "value": "The Carro Armato L6/40 was Italy's standard light tank, some three hundred of which served in Russia and North Africa. Fast and mechanically sound, its 20mm Breda cannon handles infantry and unarmoured vehicles well enough, and its thin plate keeps it very cheap. Against anything properly armoured it is helpless, and it was already obsolete the day it entered service. Immediately available from the barracks, it is best used to screen infantry, scout ahead, and hunt soft targets.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "b488a254daf65cc6",
          "value": "A Pershing with the armour it deserved. Twenty-seven were built by welding extra plate onto the front of the hull and casting a much thicker mantlet, producing the best-protected American tank to reach Europe. It keeps the 90mm gun, but the extra weight costs it what little agility the Pershing had and strained a transmission never meant to carry it. Expensive and slow, it is a breakthrough tank: it should lead an attack, going where lighter tanks cannot survive, rather than manoeuvre.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "418c9562eba32bdb",
          "value": "The heavier development of the P43, redrawn to take the 90/53 anti- aircraft gun in a turret, the same weapon that had already proved itself against armour in the desert. Built, it would have been the most powerful Italian tank of the war by a wide margin, able to defeat any medium tank at range. Only plans and a mock-up existed by September 1943. Expensive and slow to produce, it is best held back to engage enemy armour at long range, escorted against aircraft and infantry.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "1944b3285e82a9e8",
          "value": "The standard Soviet heavy anti-aircraft gun, and far too good at shooting things on the ground to be left pointing at the sky. Its 85mm shell defeats the frontal armour of most medium tanks at ranges where they cannot answer, and Soviet crews used it in that role as routinely as the Germans used their 88. It is a large, heavy piece, however, slow to bring into position and slower still to leave it. Given a good field of fire and infantry to protect it, it dominates the ground in front.",
          "add": true
        },
        {
          "key": "48cdce42a8f30fe9",
          "value": "The heaviest tank of the Entwicklung (''development'') series, and the only one of them to be started. Armed with a monstrous 150mm gun in a turret and virtually impenetrable armour, it is an alternative to the Maus: it hits harder and is faster, though slightly more fragile. It remains slow all the same, and extremely expensive: a mobile fortress that cannot defend itself against aircraft or close assault by infantry.",
          "add": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
